A||||32||PaUL||PCV valves
Z||000000||PaUL||06-17-99||02:35 PM||||For the last few days Ive been experimenting with different PCV valves. Can anyone tell me exactly how these function? I know they allow crankcase gases to be sucked into the intake but only under certain conditions. Should it be open at idle?
Z||000001||grizco||06-17-99||07:15 PM||||PaUL- The PCV valve should be open when ever the car is running. Its purpose is to releave pressure that builds up in the crankcase. Other wise the pressure will build up until it blows out seals. I had this happen to my truck when a couple of winters ago when it froze shut. It ended up blowing out the intake manifold gasket on the back of the engine. It cut down on the amount of oil changes the changes the truck needed but eventually the leak got bad enough to leak a quart of oil every couple of days. It also started to smoke you out of the cab when the oil dripped onto the hot exhaust, luckily the truck never caught fire. Unfortunately about 6 months later the motor turned a bearing during the winter and blew up. Probably had some thing to do with the 60 below temperatures we had a week earlier combined with the fact that the motor had around 200,000 miles on it with out a rebuild.
Z||000002||grizco||06-17-99||07:17 PM||||I forgot to mention that it took two years for the leak to get that bad. Before that it only used a quart a week. 
Z||000003||theogre||06-17-99||07:26 PM||||actually a PCV valve opens and closes at various times. It's primary job is to insure removal of fumes from the crank case, not to releave presure. If presure is building up you've got other problems as well, like a blocked breather tube/filter, or fried rings...<p>The PCV is operated by engine vacuum. it will open durring a certain portion of the engines range of vacuum. It will close at very high vacuum to prevent oil from being sucked into the intake manfold. When it is open it draws air into the motor thru the breather tube. This air keeps the crank case air fresh to prevent oil contamination that results in sludge and oil breakdown.<p>Exactly when the PCV valve is open varies some from engine to engine. I'm not sure if Fiero is open at Idle. Since vacuum is rather high at idle, it may not be. 
Z||000004||Carrolles||06-17-99||07:41 PM||||Welcome back Ogre.
Z||000005||Yellow85||06-17-99||08:24 PM||||When i checked intake pipe, i notice quite a lot of oil in the pipe. Is this normal?  It actually started to happen from last few month.
Z||000006||batboy||06-17-99||08:58 PM||||Yellow85, if you mean that you are finding oil in the air cleaner assembly, then this is usually due to excessive blow-by. What that means is that oil is "blowing by" the piston rings or valve guides or both. This is real common on engines with a lot of miles or hard use. What it probably means is that your engine is getting worn out and is starting to use a lot of oil. Check your engine oil level frequently if that is the case. Unfortunately, it will gradually keep getting worse and performance will begin to suffer. I once had an old beat-up work truck that burned so much oil that I had to replace the air filter about every two months because it would be soaked with oil. It also fouled a lot of spark plugs. I used to joke that it burned more oil than gas.
Z||000007||batboy||06-17-99||09:02 PM||||I've also heard that a defective PCV valve or the wrong PVC valve will sometimes siphon oil up into the air cleaner.
Z||000008||Yellow85||06-17-99||09:12 PM||||Is there anything i can do to make less oil burn instead of Engine rebuild?
Z||000009||batboy||06-17-99||09:31 PM||||I'm afraid that once an engine gets worn out, there's not much you can do except rebuild it. There is no miracle cure, even though there are some gimmicky products that claim otherwise, they are just a waste of money. One thing you can do to keep from wearing the engine out as fast (and maybe slow down the oil consumation a little) is to quit racing everyone out on the highway (I read your post in another discussion, sounds like you have a lead foot). Hard acceleration and high speed is very hard on an engine, that's why a lot of race cars go through several engines in one racing season. Top fuel dragsters sometimes even rebuild engines in between rounds.
Z||000010||Yellow85||06-17-99||10:02 PM||||Batboy,<br>I drive my car for more than 2000 miles every month. How long do you think my engine gonna last, any idea? <br>                    -Thanks!!
Z||000011||batboy||06-17-99||11:17 PM||||That's a good question and to be honest, I don't know. How much oil is it burning? If you're only putting a quart of oil in every 2,000 miles, that's not too bad. If you're adding a quart of oil every 200 miles, that's getting bad. Remember, the longer you let an engine go, the more expensive a rebuild could be and you might even ruin the block. If you're burning a lot of oil and have to wait for awhile before repairing or replacing it, I'd definitely start taking it easy on the car. The harder you drive it, the quicker it will finish wearing out and leave you stranded. Start saving up some money. You may want to do a compression check on the engine.
Z||000012||theogre||06-17-99||11:37 PM||||I have to agree with his battyness....<p>I've got 133333+ as of this evening on my 87. It doesn't burn oil yet thank god. (tho it's got a minor leak under it some place I haven't found yet.)<p>If you are regularly poluting the breather filter with oil, then your motor is getting really worn. A little oil on the breather tubing is normal, but no measurable amount should be there, and it shouldn't be poluting filters.<p>The simple test is to put in a new PCV, and make absolutly sure it's the right one. If you still get the same results the motor is on it's way south for the winter. (make sure the vacuum line to the PCV is in good shape and not blocked.)<p><br>As for me..... I'm somewhat back. the initial impact has worn off. Now I just have to figure out the rest.
Z||000013||lowCG||06-17-99||11:55 PM||||I agree that when you have to pour oil at every fillup,take a bus.<br>I always run Kendall 20/50 in my "loose" motors.The worst example was in a Mini-Cooper that had sixty thou,piston to bore clearance;clouded out with bargain priced havoline,but screamed with the 20/50.It's worth a try,I say,just have a spare motor under the workbench.
Z||000014||PaUL||06-18-99||12:02 AM||||hey all, I asked the question because I wanted to know exactly what the PCV valve does. I cant just use the correct valve for the fiero because my engine is totally modified. After looking at the 4 different ones I have, 4cyl, 350 V8, stock 2.8, and one for an unknown engine, I can say that at least 2 different variables are involved. All the valves allow some air to pass when fully closed but the amount varies widely. They also vary in the vacuum needed to close. I observed that at idle none of the valves fully closed, but instead pulsated between an open and closed state with varying frequencies. The one for the V8 switched on and off at a freq of about 3 sec. The others were much faster, the fastest being the one for the 4cyl.  I wonder if its supposed to do this at all? 
Z||000015||Stroker||06-18-99||09:05 PM||||  Paul,if I were you I'd just toss the PCV and install valve cover mounted breathers on both sides!<br>  The PCV valve may not add a significant amount of heat to the incoming air charge, but, it adds SOME heat. If you remove it altogether (providing it's legal where you live) the air will be cooler (slightly more dense) and DEFINITELY cleaner!!!<br>  And if you can get along without the EGR then you further eliminate heat in the incoming air.<br>  Just be sure to plug the vacuum lines!<br>                Have fun,  <br>                     Stroker
Z||000016||Stroker||06-18-99||09:11 PM||||  Besides what I just said, the point is you don't even need the PCV anyway!<br>  It's just another government mandated piece of junk!! The old cars just had a breather pipe coming off of the back of the block (in my humble opinion the best place for it) right under the intake manifold.
Z||000017||theogre||06-18-99||09:47 PM||||removing emmisions devices of any type from the car is a federal offence. There is no place in the U.S. this is legal for street driven cars.<p>PCV, I can assure you, is not just a federal item. Breather tubes don't induce fresh air flow thru the motor, they only vent pressure buildup. The trapped gasses in the crank case will react causing oil breakdown, deposites on internal parts, and other such things. This isn't just an emission control device.<p>What one is best for the modified motor is going to be a bit of trial and error. Personally, as long as it's moving some air, and not killing the engine doing it, it's probably not to critical.<p>When you are testing tho, you must mount the valve in it's correct position. A valve, like the normal one for pre87 4cyl, is mounted sideways. The one for the V8 may have been intended to be vertical. changing the position of the valve will change how it operates.
Z||000018||Stroker||06-18-99||09:56 PM||||  You sure are an OGRE!!<br>But you are WAY OFF on this one!!<br>  Paul,(and anyone else) doing what I recomend will LENGTHEN an engines life NOT shorten it!<br>  
Z||000019||Stroker||06-18-99||10:05 PM||||  Besides, I hope we all change our oil at least every 3,000 miles.
Z||000020||lowCG||06-19-99||12:09 AM||||Well,if your engine was so heavily modified ,it would've blown the PCV valve out and solved that one,more likely,however you  need to use a breather on one side,and a PCV on the other,with the most capacity(sounds like the 350 one),then re-jet(probably only idle jets) your carburetor to compensate for the change in effective vacume.If you can't get the right balance with the big one,go smaller until it works the way you wantThen you should stick to the same PCV valve after this to retain your adjuustments.Idle,and off idle settings determine alot in the way a car drives,mileage it gets.
Z||000021||Pretender||06-20-99||03:22 AM||||when my egr valve went out the guy at a local garage said it wasn't that much of a deal so i just plugged the vacume lines, but the check engine light kept coming on and i decided to just put a new on one to keep the light from popping up.
Z||000022||Cooter||06-20-99||09:30 AM||||Stroker and anyone else who decides to remove the PCV system, my advice is to leave it alone! Without the Positive ventilation, varnish building compounds pile up and create a sludge layer in the engine quickly. I know because I toasted a hi-performance 327 by disabling the PCV. The rebuild was costly and messy, but proved a valuable point- Dad was right and I should have listened. Yes, I know race cars have valve cover breathers, but most have a header mounted vent system that uses exhaust pulses to pull a vacume on the engine, and they rebuild their engines every week. 
Z||000023||theogre||06-20-99||11:59 PM||||Yup, I'm an ogre. But you really don't want to go there. :)<p>Stroker,<p>Check your facts and history of engines. The PCV valve has been used on practically every engine built since the yearly to midle 1960's, maybe farther back than that. Federal emisions regs didn't start until the 1974 model year. It's job is to do avoid exactly the problem Cooter describes. As far as I can tell, The PCV valve predates even seat belts which weren't required until 1965 as I recall.<p>Only on race engines, with the tremendous blowby they experience, is it still common practice to use only valve cover vents. On these motors it is also common practice to do huge ammounts of maintenance that requires constant changing of the oil.
Z||000024||PaUL||06-21-99||03:29 AM||||hey all, after trying all the different PCV valve I had, found out the best was the stock 2.8 valve. I also discovered that the engine seemed to run worst without any valve and vaccuum line plugged. I am conviced that a PCV system is needed for optimal engine performance. It seems to add a slight amount of pressure boost in the intake manifold when vacuum is very low.
Z||000025||DJRice||06-24-99||11:01 AM||||Ogre, you mentioned an phantom oil leak in your 87.  I just recently fixed an oil problem in my 87 that was kind of hard to track down because I was looking in the wrong spot.  My Oil Pressure sending unit was leaking, but it wasnt entirely obvious from the top.  Only after I disconnected it (exploratory surgery) did I discover that it was the problem.  <p>Just a heads up....
Z||000026||theogre||06-24-99||01:22 PM||||Is that fool thing up under the manifold by the oil filter? I can't quite see for sure yet either. I may just change it anyway, hell I've changed nearly every other sensor. what's another few bucks.<p>I did find one I'll fix at the next oil change,,,, the drain plug needs a new ring on it. It doesn't leak to bad, not even a quart between changes. just enough to kill the grass and be annoying. Thanks for the note.
Z||000027||DJRice||06-24-99||03:47 PM||||Actually, It is on the top, but you will have to (I had to) pull the battery out to get a wrench on it.  It is a silver cylinder type gadget about 2 inches wide with a hex shaped base to get a wrench on.  It is on the top of a small pipe that comes up from the bottom.  It has a 3 wire connector on it with a funky plastic thing to hold it on securely.  The oil was leaking out, running down the pipe and dripping from several places on the engine which made it relatively confusing to spot.  <p>Hope this helps.
